Kernel futex wait/wake table
============================

POSIX-style `futex(2)` substrate. The kernel-side state is a small
hash table of per-bucket intrusive lists; each waiting thread is
linked into one bucket keyed by the physical address it's parked on.
Lookup keys are *physical addresses* (page-aligned base + low 12-bit
offset) so MAP_SHARED futexes — same underlying frame mapped under
two different virtual addresses, possibly in two different processes
— hash to the same bucket and find each other.

Bucket count is small but never linear in `MAX_THREADS`: the wake
path walks one bucket's chain, not every thread slot. The chain
itself is intrusive (per-thread `futex_next`), so insert and remove
are O(1) once you have the bucket.

The actual `*uaddr == val` compare, the timeout setup, the EINTR
loop, and the user-space arg parsing all live in
`oskit/posix/futex.lsysl`. This file owns only the kernel data
structure plus the four primitives the shim layer needs:

  - `kernel_futex_wait_enter(pa, bitset)` — insert current thread
    into the bucket for `pa`. Sets per-thread `futex_addr_pa` /
    `futex_bitset`.
  - `kernel_futex_wait_exit()` — remove current thread from its
    bucket (idempotent — safe to call on every exit path).
  - `kernel_futex_wake(pa, max, bitset) -> int` — wake up to `max`
    waiters parked on `pa` whose bitset intersects `bitset`. Wake
    means: unlink, clear `futex_addr_pa`, call `notify_send(tid, 0)`
    so the waiter's `sleep_or_notify` loop in the shim returns
    promptly. Returns the count actually woken.
  - `kernel_futex_requeue(from_pa, to_pa, wake_n, requeue_n) -> int`
    — wake `wake_n` from `from_pa`, then move up to `requeue_n` of
    the remaining waiters to `to_pa`'s bucket. Used by musl's
    `pthread_cond_*` (`FUTEX_REQUEUE` / `FUTEX_CMP_REQUEUE`).
    Returns total threads affected (woken + requeued).

    module oskit.kernel

    import oskit.services.{notify_send}


Bucket count and hash. 16 buckets is more than slix's current
MAX_THREADS=32 needs but the size is also load-bearing for the
hash distribution: with 16 buckets every page-aligned waiter
on the same page collides, which is exactly what `FUTEX_WAKE`
wants (wake-all on a page reaches every per-word futex on that
page in one walk). Pure power-of-2 lets the hash collapse to a
mask.

The shift-by-4 mirrors what Linux does: futex compare addresses
have to be 32-bit-aligned, so the low two bits of the PA are
always zero; shifting by 4 spreads the next four address bits
across the bucket index.

    val FUTEX_BUCKETS = 16
    val FUTEX_BUCKET_MASK = 15

    private var _futex_head: [16]int
    private var _futex_init_done = 0

    private futex_hash(pa: u64) -> int
        int((pa >> u64(4))) & FUTEX_BUCKET_MASK

    private futex_ensure_init()
        if _futex_init_done != 0
            return
        for i in 0..<FUTEX_BUCKETS
            _futex_head[i] = -1
        _futex_init_done = 1


Insert the current thread at the head of its bucket. The caller
must have already set `futex_addr_pa` / `futex_bitset` to the
target values; this routine only does the chain insertion. A
thread is "on a futex" exactly when `futex_addr_pa != 0`.

    kernel_futex_wait_enter(pa: u64, bitset: u32)
        futex_ensure_init()
        threads[current_thread].futex_addr_pa = pa
        threads[current_thread].futex_bitset = bitset
        val b = futex_hash(pa)
        threads[current_thread].futex_next = _futex_head[b]
        _futex_head[b] = current_thread


Predicate the shim layer uses to detect "was I woken explicitly
or did I time out / take a signal." After a successful
`kernel_futex_wake`, the parked thread's `futex_addr_pa` is
zeroed, so a non-zero value means we're still on a bucket and
the wakeup must have come from elsewhere (timeout, signal).

    kernel_futex_self_parked() -> int
        if threads[current_thread].futex_addr_pa == u64(0)
            return 0
        1


Remove `tid` from whatever bucket it's on. Idempotent: if
`futex_addr_pa == 0` the thread isn't on the table and this is a
cheap no-op, which matters because shim exit paths call us
unconditionally on every wake/timeout/EINTR and the thread-
termination path calls us defensively on every kill so a thread
that died mid-park doesn't leave a stale pointer in a bucket
(another process's wake would later find the terminated tid and
post a notification to a freed slot — harmless today, broken once
slot reuse happens fast enough to outrun a long wake walk).

    kernel_futex_wait_exit_tid(tid: int)
        if threads[tid].futex_addr_pa == u64(0)
            return
        val b = futex_hash(threads[tid].futex_addr_pa)
        if _futex_head[b] == tid
            _futex_head[b] = threads[tid].futex_next
        else
            var prev = _futex_head[b]
            while prev >= 0 && threads[prev].futex_next != tid
                prev = threads[prev].futex_next
            if prev >= 0
                threads[prev].futex_next = threads[tid].futex_next
        threads[tid].futex_addr_pa = u64(0)
        threads[tid].futex_bitset = u32(0)
        threads[tid].futex_next = -1

    kernel_futex_wait_exit()
        kernel_futex_wait_exit_tid(current_thread)


Wake up to `max` waiters on bucket `pa` whose bitset intersects
`bitset` (use `0xFFFFFFFF` for plain `FUTEX_WAKE`; the bitset
filter is only meaningful for `FUTEX_WAKE_BITSET`). Returns the
number of threads actually woken.

Each wake clears the waiter's `futex_addr_pa` (so a concurrent
`kernel_futex_wait_exit` from another path is a no-op), unlinks
it from the bucket, and posts a notification — the waiter's
shim loop is sitting in `sleep_or_notify(1)`, and `notify_send`
makes that return on the next 10 ms boundary at the latest.

    kernel_futex_wake(pa: u64, max: int, bitset: u32) -> int
        futex_ensure_init()
        if max <= 0
            return 0
        val b = futex_hash(pa)
        var woken = 0
        var prev = -1
        var cur = _futex_head[b]
        while cur >= 0 && woken < max
            val nxt = threads[cur].futex_next
            if threads[cur].futex_addr_pa == pa
                if (threads[cur].futex_bitset & bitset) != u32(0)
                    if prev < 0
                        _futex_head[b] = nxt
                    else
                        threads[prev].futex_next = nxt
                    threads[cur].futex_addr_pa = u64(0)
                    threads[cur].futex_bitset = u32(0)
                    threads[cur].futex_next = -1
                    notify_send(cur, 0)
                    woken += 1
                    cur = nxt
                else
                    prev = cur
                    cur = nxt
            else
                prev = cur
                cur = nxt
        woken


Move up to `requeue_n` waiters from `from_pa`'s bucket to
`to_pa`'s bucket, after first waking up to `wake_n` of them.
musl uses `FUTEX_REQUEUE` in `pthread_cond_signal` /
`_broadcast`: it wakes one (so the woken thread can run the
condvar protocol and grab the mutex), and requeues the rest
onto the mutex's wait queue so they don't all wake up and
contend for it. The return value is `woken + requeued`.

`FUTEX_CMP_REQUEUE` adds a `*from_uaddr == val3` precondition;
that check is the shim's responsibility (the kernel never sees
the user pointer here), and on a mismatch the shim returns
`-EAGAIN` without entering this routine.

    kernel_futex_requeue(from_pa: u64, to_pa: u64, wake_n: int, requeue_n: int) -> int
        futex_ensure_init()
        var woken = kernel_futex_wake(from_pa, wake_n, u32(0xFFFFFFFF))
        if requeue_n <= 0
            return woken
        val from_b = futex_hash(from_pa)
        val to_b   = futex_hash(to_pa)
        var moved = 0
        var prev = -1
        var cur = _futex_head[from_b]
        while cur >= 0 && moved < requeue_n
            val nxt = threads[cur].futex_next
            if threads[cur].futex_addr_pa == from_pa
                if prev < 0
                    _futex_head[from_b] = nxt
                else
                    threads[prev].futex_next = nxt
                threads[cur].futex_addr_pa = to_pa
                threads[cur].futex_next = _futex_head[to_b]
                _futex_head[to_b] = cur
                moved += 1
                cur = nxt
            else
                prev = cur
                cur = nxt
        woken + moved
